# A raised global narrates forever

**DONE.** From `tasks/095`, which measured it. Fixed 2026-09-05.

## The defect

A process that has once called `subscribe_logs` or
`subscribe_process_logs` with a level above lvlInfo prints the
DAEMON's debug lines on stderr for the rest of its life - to every
caller, subscribed or not, on every thread.

`tasks/095` counted 1052 lines on an unsubscribed caller, from 1051
worker ops, deterministically across 12 repetitions.

## Why no gate catches it

The level is erased on the wire.
`worker-protocol-connection.cc:75` reads a `STDERR_NEXT` frame and
calls `printError` on its text. A daemon `debug()` therefore reaches
the client as **lvlError**, which is 0 and passes every gate this
repository has and every gate nix has. `tasks/095` holds the full
chain.

So this cannot be fixed downstream. Nothing downstream still knows
what the level was.

## The fix

Lower `nix::verbosity` when the subscription that raised it goes.

**Not to lvlInfo.** To the WIDEST level any LIVE subscription still
needs, or lvlInfo when there is none. Lowering to lvlInfo would
silently stop a still-subscribed thread's records, which is a silent
skip and this repository's named failure mode.

`packages/huggorm/huggorm/server.py` already computes exactly this
one layer up, in `_widest(readers)`, and re-opens a subscription when
a joiner wants more than the current one. The C++ needs the same
rule over live subscriptions: a count per level, or a max over a
registry - the shape is a decision, the rule is not.

## What lowering cannot reach

**A connection already open keeps the level it was given.**
`setOptions` runs once, at handshake. So a `Store` opened while a
vomit subscription was live goes on receiving vomit from its daemon
until it is closed, whatever the global says afterwards.

That is a bounded remainder and it should be written down rather than
hidden: lowering fixes every connection opened after it, which is the
common case (subscribe, work, unsubscribe, later open a store).

Whether to go further - reconnect, or refuse to raise once a
connection is open - is a separate question and probably not worth
it. Say what the limit is.

## What was built

`VerbosityDemand` in `huggorm_decl/cpp/logging.hpp`. Eight counters,
one per `nix::Verbosity`, under one mutex. `add` and `drop` each
reconcile: the widest level with a live holder wins, and the floor is
whatever `nix::verbosity` held before anything touched it - read once
in the constructor rather than written as `lvlInfo`.

A COUNT PER LEVEL rather than a maximum, because a maximum cannot be
undone. Two subscriptions at talkative and one at debug, and the
debug one leaving has to return talkative rather than the floor.

`ThreadLevel` owns the pairing for a thread. `subscribe_logs` calls
`set`, `unsubscribe_logs` calls `clear`, and the destructor calls the
same `release` - so a thread that exits while still subscribed gives
its level back.

`set_process_demand` is the same for the process-wide sink. It holds
the number itself, because `default_verbosity` reads `lvlInfo` both
when nobody is subscribed and when a sink asked for exactly
`lvlInfo`, and a release has to give back what was taken.

It takes a MUTEX, and an atomic exchange was written first. The
exchange races: B reads A's level and drops it before A's add has
landed, the guarded decrement finds a zero and does nothing, A's add
then has no owner, and the gate stays up after both unsubscribe - a
phantom holder, which is the defect this task removes, reintroduced
by the fix. Unreachable through the rpc, which admits one
process-logs stream at a time. An in-process caller has no such rule,
so the mutex is the answer rather than the argument that nobody will
do it.

## What was got wrong, and what refuted it

The first version left `ThreadLevel` a plain aggregate and gave it a
destructor. The call site then read:

    chosen = {.own = true, .level = wanted};

which builds a TEMPORARY, copy-assigns it, and destroys the
temporary - running the new destructor on a copy that claims to own
the level, and dropping the demand that had just been added. Net
zero.

Every per-thread subscription silently failed to raise nix's gate.
The suite said so immediately, in three gates at once, including the
one `tasks/089` had already written. That is what a gate is for: the
reasoning was wrong, and the reasoning is not what caught it.

The fix is not "be careful". `ThreadLevel` is a class now, its copy
assignment is DELETED, and `set`/`clear`/`~ThreadLevel` are the only
things that touch the demand. The compiler rejects the line that was
wrong.

## The gates, each proved by breaking it

Three, in `test_logs.py`, one perturbation each:

1. `test_the_gate_goes_back_down_when_a_subscription_ends`.
   Perturbation: `release` never calls `drop`. **2 fail.**
2. `test_the_gate_stays_up_for_a_subscription_that_is_still_live`.
   Perturbation: `drop` sets the floor directly - the naive fix this
   task rejects. **1 fails**, and it is this one; the gate above
   still passes, which is what makes the pair discriminating.
3. `test_a_daemon_told_to_narrate_is_told_to_stop`, `live`.
   Perturbation: `unsubscribe_process_logs` keeps its demand.
   The hermetic half fails gate 2; the LIVE gate fails with
   `performing daemon worker op: 1` on captured stderr, which is the
   defect itself.

Gate 3 needs `capfd`, not `capsys`: the write is a C++
`writeToStderr` on descriptor 2, and `capsys` only replaces Python's
objects. It reads a path that is already in the store rather than
adding one - every worker op narrates, so nothing has to be written,
and `tasks/062` is why a suite does not write to the real store when
it has a choice.

Suite: 417 passed, 0 failed, everything including `live`.

## The remainder, stated

A daemon connection already open keeps the level `setOptions` gave
it. So a `Store` opened while a vomit subscription was live goes on
receiving vomit until it is closed, whatever the global says
afterwards. Lowering fixes every connection opened after it, which is
the common case: subscribe, work, unsubscribe, later open a store.

Going further - reconnecting, or refusing to raise once a connection
is open - is not worth it, and the limit is written into
`subscribe_process_logs`'s docstring where a caller reads it.

Opened 2026-09-05, from `tasks/095`. Done the same day.
